{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh has resolved the unmet verification condition, and it changes what this change can honestly claim.\n\nTHE FACT. Hugh researched the client that prompted this request. The version of kiro he was using has no support for structuredContent at all, whether or not a tool advertises an outputSchema. So the reported symptom was not \"structuredContent is invisible because outputSchema is missing\" — that client would not have surfaced the payload either way.\n\nWHAT THAT MEANS FOR THE CONDITION. The request asked me to verify against a real client that structuredContent is now recognised. I reported that unmet and asked codex to rule whether it blocks. It is not merely unmet — it is unsatisfiable: hugh's words are that we do not know any environment that would positively confirm it. A condition no available environment can discharge should not sit on the row as though someone were failing to do it.\n\nWHAT THAT MEANS FOR THE CHANGE. Hugh's judgement is that the schema inclusion is worthwhile regardless, and I agree, but the justification has to move. The premise in the request — that some clients only recognise structuredContent when an outputSchema is advertised — is not what was happening in the case that prompted it. What stands on its own is narrower and still real: the MCP specification provides outputSchema for exactly this declaration, gitseq returns structuredContent and said nothing about its shape, and a client that does gate on the schema's presence now has what it needs. That is a conformance and interoperability argument rather than a fix for an observed breakage.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO ABOUT MY OWN ARTIFACT. 2008eba8 describes the change accurately but inherits the request's framing of why. If codex's verdict does not already require a new head, I would rather republish it with the justification corrected than leave a filing whose motivation the originator has since disproved. Saying \"this fixes a client that could not have been fixed by it\" is precisely the shape of overstatement this lane has been correcting all day, and it is not improved by the fact that I inherited the sentence rather than invented it.\n\nNO CODE CHANGE IS IMPLIED. The schema, the loop that attaches it, the test and the mutation evidence are all unaffected. This is about what the record claims, not what the code does.","body":{"dead_basis_override":"true"}}